{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T07:47:46Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-09T13:17:46+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-09T13:17:46+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/college/College%20sem%205/bie%20artifacts/BIE_complete_answer_bank.md`\nTotal Lines: 1051\nTotal Bytes: 59394\nShowing lines 1 to 800\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # BIE Complete Answer Bank — Print & Learn\n2: ### Basic of Industrial Engineering (BE05000321) | GTU Mid-Sem 30 Marks\n3: ### Questions Covered: 25 | Excluded: Therbligs, String Diagram\n4: \n5: ---\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # CHAPTER 1: LOCATION SELECTION AND PLANT LAYOUT\n9: \n10: ---\n11: \n12: ## Q1. What are the different types of plant layout? Differentiate between Product and Process layout. [7 Marks]\n13: \n14: **Definition:** Plant layout refers to the arrangement of machines, equipment, work stations, storage areas, aisles, and common areas within a factory building to achieve the most efficient flow of materials at the lowest cost.\n15: \n16: ### Types of Plant Layout:\n17: \n18: **1. Product Layout (Line Layout):**\n19: - Machines and equipment are arranged in the sequence of operations required to manufacture the product.\n20: - The raw material enters from one end and the finished product comes out from the other end.\n21: - Used for mass production and continuous production.\n22: - Example: Automobile assembly line, bottling plant.\n23: \n24: **2. Process Layout (Functional Layout):**\n25: - Machines of the same type or performing the same function are grouped together in one department.\n26: - The product moves from one department to another according to the sequence of operations.\n27: - Used for job order production and batch production.\n28: - Example: Hospital (X-ray dept, Surgery dept, OPD), Machine shop (Lathe section, Milling section).\n29: \n30: **3. Fixed Position Layout:**\n31: - The major product remains in a fixed position and work\n<truncated 44172 bytes>\n68: **Definition:** A quality audit is a systematic, independent, and documented examination to determine whether quality activities and related results comply with planned arrangements and whether these arrangements are implemented effectively and are suitable to achieve objectives.\n769: \n770: **Types of Quality Audit:**\n771: 1. **Product Audit** — Examines the product for conformance to specifications.\n772: 2. **Process Audit** — Examines the process for compliance with procedures.\n773: 3. **System Audit** — Examines the entire quality management system.\n774: \n775: **Purpose:**\n776: - Verify compliance with quality standards (ISO 9001)\n777: - Identify areas for improvement\n778: - Check effectiveness of corrective actions\n779: - Build customer confidence\n780: \n781: ---\n782: \n783: ## Q22. What is Six Sigma? Explain the DMAIC principle and methodology. [4-7 Marks]\n784: \n785: ### Definition of Six Sigma:\n786: \n787: Six Sigma is a disciplined, data-driven approach and methodology for eliminating defects in any process — from manufacturing to transactional and service processes. The goal is to achieve near-perfection by reducing defects to no more than **3.4 defects per million opportunities (DPMO)**.\n788: \n789: The term \"Six Sigma\" refers to 6 standard deviations from the mean in a normal distribution curve, which statistically means 99.99966% of products are defect-free.\n790: \n791: **Developed by:** Motorola in 1986. Later adopted by General Electric (GE) under Jack Welch.\n792: \n793: ### Concept:\n794: - Sigma (σ) is a measure of variation/spread in a process.\n795: - Higher sigma level = fewer defects = better quality.\n796: - 1 Sigma = 690,000 DPMO (31% yield) — very poor\n797: - 3 Sigma = 66,807 DPMO (93.3% yield) — average\n798: - 6 Sigma = 3.4 DPMO (99.99966% yield) — near perfection\n799: \n800: ### DMAIC Methodology:\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
