{"step_index":10,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T07:48:16Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-09T13:18:16+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-09T13:18:16+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/college/College%20sem%205/bie%20artifacts/BIE_complete_answer_bank.md`\nTotal Lines: 1051\nTotal Bytes: 59394\nShowing lines 801 to 1051\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n801: \n802: DMAIC is the core methodology of Six Sigma for improving existing processes. It stands for:\n803: \n804: **1. D — Define:**\n805: - Define the problem, project goals, and customer requirements (CTQ — Critical to Quality).\n806: - Identify the process to be improved.\n807: - Create a project charter with scope, objectives, team members, and timeline.\n808: \n809: **2. M — Measure:**\n810: - Measure the current performance of the process.\n811: - Collect data on defects, cycle time, and key process variables.\n812: - Establish the baseline sigma level.\n813: - Tools: Data collection, check sheets, Pareto chart.\n814: \n815: **3. A — Analyze:**\n816: - Analyze the data to identify root causes of defects and process variation.\n817: - Find the gap between current performance and desired performance.\n818: - Tools: Cause-and-effect diagram (Fishbone), 5-Why analysis, scatter diagrams, hypothesis testing.\n819: \n820: **4. I — Improve:**\n821: - Develop and implement solutions to eliminate root causes.\n822: - Design experiments to test proposed solutions.\n823: - Optimize the process.\n824: - Tools: Brainstorming, design of experiments (DOE), pilot testing.\n825: \n826: **5. C — Control:**\n827: - Control the improved process to sustain the gains.\n828: - Implement control charts and monitoring systems.\n829: - Standardize the new process and document